      "text": "To solve the problem \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\), we start by expressing everything in terms of a common trigonometric function.\n\nFirst, recall the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\\). From this identity, we can express \\(\\cos 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 80^\\circ\\) in terms of each other.\n\nLet's rewrite the given expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression with a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nNow, we rearrange the terms:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity for the tangent function:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3} \\tan 80^\\circ}{\\tan 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nNow, we know that:\n\\[\n\\text{cosecant } 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sin 80^\\circ} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ - \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
