      "text": "\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nI. Compute the original quantity.\nWe can find two trigonometric identities to relate $\\cos(80^\\circ)$ and $\\sin(80^\\circ)$;\nLet $x = 80^\\circ$.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos^2(x) + \\sin^2(x) &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^2(x) + \\sqrt{3}\\sin^2(x) &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^2(x) &= 1 - \\sqrt{3}\\sin^2(x) \\\\\n\\sqrt{3}\\sin^2(x) &= 1 - \\cos^2(x) \\\\\n\\sqrt{3}\\sin^2(x) &= \\sin^2(x) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nLet $\\sin^2(x) = a$.\nThis implies that $\\sin^2(x) = \\frac{a}{1+\\sqrt{3}}$.\nTherefore,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin(80^\\circ) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\\n\\cos(80^\\circ) &= \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{align*}\nSo,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\text{cosine of 80 degrees}} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\text{sin of 80 degrees}} &= \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\\n&= 2 - 2 \\\\\n&= \\boxed{0}.\n\\end{align*}\nThis indicates that the original quantity equals 0, demonstrating the underlying principles of trigonometry.",
